This is just a modification to an existing test case (no real code changes).
a) Turn on antialiasing
b) change image validation;
     * keep existing checks on the line style color samples
     * add a new check to make sure there is text on the image (verifies theres a lot of very dark pixels)

This was done because the Mac M1 (ARM-based java) doesn't seem to be able to draw non-antialiased text.
The Mac M1 (with X86_64 based java) can draw both antialiased and antialiased text.

Here's a simple code to show that;
```
@org.junit.Test
    public void test_aliasing_text() throws Exception {
        BufferedImage renderedLabel = new BufferedImage(100, 100, BufferedImage.TYPE_INT_ARGB);
        Graphics2D rlg = renderedLabel.createGraphics();
        rlg.setColor(Color.RED);
        rlg.setFont(new Font("TimesRoman", Font.PLAIN, 16));

        //change here to turn aliasing on/off
        rlg.setRenderingHint(
                // RenderingHints.KEY_TEXT_ANTIALIASING,RenderingHints.VALUE_TEXT_ANTIALIAS_ON
                RenderingHints.KEY_TEXT_ANTIALIASING, RenderingHints.VALUE_TEXT_ANTIALIAS_OFF);

        rlg.drawString("Hello World", 0, 10);
        rlg.dispose();
        javax.imageio.ImageIO.write(renderedLabel, "png", new java.io.File("out.png"));
    }
```
